* Processing array contents

- Processing array elements is no different than processing other
  variables. This includes use of pre- and post-increment operations.

- Example: Increase score values in an array
  #+begin_src C++ :main yes :includes <iostream> <cstdlib> <string> :namespaces std :results output :exports both :noweb yes
    int score[] {7,8,9,10,11};
    cout << score[2] << endl;
    cout << ++score[2] << endl;
    cout << score[4]++ << endl;
    cout << score[4] << endl;    
  #+end_src

  #+RESULTS:
  : 9
  : 10
  : 11
  : 12

- An easy mistake is to confuse operations on the value with
  operations on the subscript:
  #+begin_example C++
    count = 1;
    value[count--];  // value[1] -> value[0]
    value[count]--;  // value[1] -> value[1] - 1
  #+end_example

* Assigning arrays

- Does the following code work? Why or why not?
  #+begin_src C++ :main yes :includes <iostream> <cstdlib> <string> :namespaces std :results output :exports both :noweb yes
    const int SIZE {4};
    int oldValues[SIZE] {10,100,200,300};
    int newValues[SIZE];
    newValues = oldValues;
  #+end_src

  #+RESULTS:

- Answer:
  #+begin_quote
  The only way to assign one C-style array to another is to
  assign the individual elements in the array.
  #+end_quote

  #+begin_src C++ :main yes :includes <iostream> <cstdlib> <string> :namespaces std :results output :exports both     
    const int SIZE {4};
    int oldValues[SIZE] {10,100,200,300};
    int newValues[SIZE];

    for (int count=0; count<SIZE; count++)
      {
        newValues[count] = oldValues[count];
        cout << newValues[count] << " ";
      }
  #+end_src

  #+RESULTS:
  : 10 100 200 300 

- Explanation:
  #+begin_quote
  The reason why the array assignment does not work is that you
  would have to change the STARTING MEMORY ADDRESS of the array.

  Using =newValues= in code means that the compiler sees:
  =&newValues[0]=

  =newValues = oldValues= is seen as 8012 = 8024
  #+end_quote
  #+attr_html: :width 600px :float nil:
  [[../img/array_copy.png]]

- If =num= is an array of =int= numbers, what will =cout << num;= print?
  #+begin_src C++ :main yes :includes <iostream> <cstdlib> <string> :namespaces std :results output :exports both :noweb yes
    int num[] {10,20,30,40,50};
    cout << num;  // this is the starting address
    cout << '\n' << &num[1]; // one int = 4 bytes forward
  #+end_src

  #+RESULTS:
  : 0x7ffd5ea5b350
  : 0x7ffd5ea5b354

- Explanation:
  #+begin_quote
  Notice that the address of =num[1]= is 4 bytes = length of an =int=
  in memory forward from the address of =num[0]=.
  #+end_quote  
  
